---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Wildlife Conservation & Community Empowerment Charter"
  description="Discover how Demo Safaris Uganda models support for the Uganda Wildlife Authority 20% community revenue share, frontline ranger welfare, Batwa cultural preservation, and reforestation buffers."
>

  <!-- Hero Header -->
  <section class="bg-[#0b1c15] text-white py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 z-0">
      <img
        src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1575550959106-5a7defe28b56?auto=format&fit=crop&w=2000&q=80"
        alt="Lush rainforest canopy in Uganda"
        class="w-full h-full object-cover object-center brightness-[0.4]"
      />
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-[#0e231b] via-[#0b1c15]/60 to-black/50"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="relative z-10 max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center space-y-4">
      <div class="inline-block px-3 py-1 text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-wider uppercase bg-[#16382c]/80 text-[#dfb674] rounded border border-[#c89b53]/30">
        Sustainable Tourism Ethos
      </div>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-5xl md:text-6xl font-bold leading-tight">
        Conservation Beyond Words
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-sm sm:text-base text-[#ded6c4] font-light leading-relaxed">
        For true wilderness travel to endure, local frontier communities must be the primary beneficiaries of wildlife protection. Here is how our expeditions support Ugandan habitats and people.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 1: The Four Pillars of Our Conservation Charter -->
  <section class="py-20 max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
      
      <!-- Pillar 1: UWA 20% Revenue Share -->
      <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-xl border border-[#ded7c8] shadow-sm space-y-4 fade-in-view">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded bg-[#142c22] text-[#c89b53] flex items-center justify-center font-serif font-bold text-xl">
          01
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-[#14261f]">
          Uganda Wildlife Authority 20% Community Fund
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
          By Ugandan parliamentary law, 20% of all national park entry gate receipts are distributed directly to frontline parish councils bordering the reserves. When you purchase permits and enter parks through our guild, your fees construct clean gravity water flow systems, maternity wards, and solar primary classrooms in communities like Buhoma, Rubuguri, and Kasenyi.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 2: Ranger Welfare & Patrol Support -->
      <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-xl border border-[#ded7c8] shadow-sm space-y-4 fade-in-view fade-in-delay-1">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded bg-[#142c22] text-[#c89b53] flex items-center justify-center font-serif font-bold text-xl">
          02
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-[#14261f]">
          Frontline Ranger Support & Field Gear
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
          The men and women of the Uganda Wildlife Authority ranger corps spend weeks on end living in remote bivouacs, facing armed poachers, snare lines, and extreme weather. Our guild actively supplements ranger outposts in Bwindi, Ishasha, and Kidepo with heavy-duty all-weather boots, solar battery chargers, water filters, and extended field rations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 3: Batwa Indigenous Empowerment -->
      <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-xl border border-[#ded7c8] shadow-sm space-y-4 fade-in-view fade-in-delay-2">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded bg-[#142c22] text-[#c89b53] flex items-center justify-center font-serif font-bold text-xl">
          03
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-[#14261f]">
          Batwa Cultural Heritage Preservation
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
          The Batwa lived as traditional hunter-gatherers inside Bwindi for millennia before the forest became a national park in 1991. We partner with Batwa community associations on ancestral clan lands outside park boundaries, ensuring elders receive fair direct compensation for sharing their unmatched knowledge of medicinal plants, forest botany, and honey collection.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 4: Plastic Eradication & Reforestation -->
      <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-xl border border-[#ded7c8] shadow-sm space-y-4 fade-in-view fade-in-delay-3">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded bg-[#142c22] text-[#c89b53] flex items-center justify-center font-serif font-bold text-xl">
          04
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-[#14261f]">
          Zero Single-Use Plastic Fleet Guarantee
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
          Every custom 4x4 safari cruiser is equipped with an onboard, food-grade stainless steel chilled water dispenser. Every guest is gifted a commemorative insulated steel canteen upon airport arrival. This simple protocol eliminates more than 14,000 single-use plastic bottles from entering Uganda's pristine ecosystems annually.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Indigenous Tree Buffer Initiative -->
  <section class="bg-[#f3efe6] py-20 border-y border-[#ded7c8]">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-12 items-center">
        
        <div class="lg:col-span-6 space-y-5 fade-in-view">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#c89b53] block">
            Habitat Restoration
          </span>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-[#14261f]">
            The Bwindi Highland Reforestation Buffer
          </h2>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
            One of the primary friction points in mountain gorilla conservation is crop-raiding along park boundaries. When wild gorillas roam into local community potato and banana gardens, conflicts can arise.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
            In partnership with the Kigezi Agroforestry Guild, we fund the planting of a permanent tea and unpalatable indigenous shrub buffer zone. Gorillas will not eat tea bushes, creating a natural living fence that protects community crops while preventing retaliatory snaring. Over 35,000 indigenous saplings have been planted since 2019.
          </p>
          <div class="pt-2">
            <a
              href="/safaris"
              class="inline-flex items-center space-x-1.5 px-6 py-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#0e231b] bg-[#c89b53] hover:bg-[#d8af6e] rounded transition-colors shadow-sm"
            >
              <span>Explore Conservation Expeditions</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M14 5l7 7m0 0l-7 7m7-7H3" />
              </svg>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="lg:col-span-6 fade-in-view fade-in-delay-1">
          <div class="relative rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-[#dcd6c8] shadow-lg">
            <img
              src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540573133985-87b6da6d54a9?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80"
              alt="Ugandan rainforest restoration"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            />
            <div class="absolute bottom-0 inset-x-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-black/80 to-transparent p-6 text-white">
              <span class="text-xs uppercase font-bold text-[#dfb674] tracking-wider block">Boundary Zone</span>
              <p class="text-xs text-[#ded6c4] mt-1">Indigenous planting site in the Rubuguri corridor, Kisoro District.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: Guest Code of Respectful Travel -->
  <section class="py-20 max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-16 fade-in-view">
      <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#c89b53] block mb-2">
        Ethical Guidelines
      </span>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-[#14261f]">
        Our Ethical Wildlife Charter
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-[#615a4f]">
        How we conduct every private drive, boat cruise, and forest walk across Uganda.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg border border-[#ded7c8] shadow-sm space-y-3 fade-in-view">
        <h4 class="font-serif text-lg font-bold text-[#14261f]">Never Crowd Animals</h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
          We do not box in predators or surround lion prides with multiple vehicles. If another car is already parked at a sighting, our guides position quietly at an ethical perimeter or seek alternate wildlife corridors.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg border border-[#ded7c8] shadow-sm space-y-3 fade-in-view fade-in-delay-1">
        <h4 class="font-serif text-lg font-bold text-[#14261f]">Strict Track Discipline</h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
          We strictly follow designated park tracks and never drive off-road on sensitive grasslands in Queen Elizabeth, Murchison, or Kidepo, preserving vulnerable root systems and ground-nesting bird clutches.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg border border-[#ded7c8] shadow-sm space-y-3 fade-in-view fade-in-delay-2">
        <h4 class="font-serif text-lg font-bold text-[#14261f]">Cultural Dignity</h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-[#544e45] leading-relaxed">
          We reject staged, exploitative human safaris. Community interactions with Batwa and Karamojong pastoralists are conducted as respectful, unscripted cultural dialogues where local elders set the terms.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>

